# Drafted by Gemini (R20), edited. R20 side quest: Doris Writes Poetry. Ambrose Quill finds a nameless poem better than his own; the party finds the rest in Doris's desk and, when the charm lifts, brings him to her.

~ start
Narrator: Ambrose Quill stands upon a creaking crate, declaiming with grand, hopeless passion to a torch-lit mob that has paused its march to listen.
ambrose_quill [smile]: Some nameless genius pinned this verse to the notice board, and it puts all my tragedies to shame! I am furious, friends, and I must find this secret master.
* Show us the poem. -> read
* We will find your poet. -> accept
* We have other troubles right now. -> END

~ read
ambrose_quill [sad]: "The rain comes down on Wickermoor, the peat is black and deep. The stones remember who we were, the heather keeps our sleep."
Narrator: Quill lets the paper fall to his side and says nothing more. It is a good poem, plain as a hearthstone and twice as heavy.
-> start

~ accept
set quill_asked
quest doris_poetry asked
ambrose_quill [smile]: A noble errand in dark times! The lettering is square and the paper bears the constable's watermark, though I accuse no one.
-> END

~ progress
if flag.golub_dead and flag.quill_poem_found and not flag.rk_poetry_met
ambrose_quill [smile]: Now that the town has its wits back, our hidden laureate may be approached. I have prepared a modest critique for the occasion.
* Come with us to the constable's station. -> go_station
* Wait here a little longer. -> END
endif
if flag.quill_poem_found
ambrose_quill [weary]: Let us hold off until the neighbours put down their torches. I have little appetite for reciting verse to a baying posse.
-> END
endif
ambrose_quill [sly]: Have you any word of my rival in rhyme? If I were a betting bard, I would look inside the constable's desk.
-> END

~ go_station
Narrator: You walk together through the damp lane toward the station, while Quill murmurs his prepared remarks under his breath.
move wickermoor_constable_station

~ drawer
Narrator: The heavy oak drawer sticks in its frame, then yields with a dry squeak. Inside lies a neat stack of square sheets tied with an old bootlace.
* [Sleight of Hand DC 10] Ease the drawer open quietly. -> found | found
* Pry the lock with a knife. -> found
* Leave the desk alone. -> END

~ found
set quill_poem_found
give rk_doris_poems
if flag.quill_asked
quest doris_poetry poem_found
endif
Narrator: Flipping through the stack, you find twenty poems of rain-soaked moors, a lost sister, and the Patient Lady, written in the exact square hand of every village warning ticket. They are remarkably good.
* Keep the bundle to show Ambrose Quill. -> keep
* [ends:doris_poetry] Burn them in the stove to keep her secret safe. -> burn

~ keep
Narrator: You pull the bootlace tight in a clean knot and slide the bundle beneath your coat.
-> END

~ burn
quest doris_poetry burned
take rk_doris_poems
Narrator: The iron stove takes the paper with a hungry crackle. Each sheet glows bright for a heartbeat, its dark ink standing sharp against the flame before crumbling to grey ash.
-> END

~ doris_station
appear ambrose_quill
Narrator: Constable Doris Squire sits behind her blotter with her cap set straight. Ambrose Quill lingers in the doorway, cradling the bundle of verse like a newborn child.
ambrose_quill [smile]: I have read all twenty, Constable, and the third is the finest verse I have heard in ten years. I would be honoured to print it under your name.
doris_squire [afraid]: It's not very good.
ambrose_quill [sly]: That, my dear Constable, is the very thing a true poet says when the work is splendid indeed.
set rk_poetry_met
quest doris_poetry met
doris_squire [smile]: Leave the stack on the blotter, speak a word of this to no one, and come back on Thursday if you must.
-> END
